big: Ezekiel
lede: A priest deported before the city fell, writing from a river in Babylonia about a God who left the building. Also the book that uses a woman's violated body as its central image of a nation's guilt, twice and at length.
calls:
k: Why this is a focused study, for now
Nine pages hold the exile setting, the departing glory, the symbolic performances and the problem of chapters 16 and 23. If any of these becomes structural to TKC, the spread is promoted. It's the strongest promotion candidate in the queue.
||
k: Content note
Page 3 deals with sexual violence, public exposure as punishment and a woman's body used as a metaphor for national guilt. Skipping it is a legitimate choice. Skipping it without knowing isn't.
/end

@page rh="EZEKIEL · 1 OF 5 · THE BOOK AND THE DEPORTATION" m="EZEK 1.1–3; 2 KGS 24–25; JER 1.1; EZEK 1–24; EZEK 33–48; GREENBERG, EZEKIEL; TKC: TL · DOM"
# What it is
rec:
The man | A priest, son of Buzi, among the deportees by the river Chebar in Babylonia. He isn't the only prophet of priestly family, since Jeremiah comes from the priests of Anathoth. What's distinctive is the combination, a priest whose whole recorded ministry happens in exile, away from the sanctuary he was trained to serve. It shapes everything the book does with temple, purity and space.
The setting | Babylon takes Jerusalem's elite in stages. Ezekiel goes in an early wave, in 597 BCE, about a decade before the city and temple are destroyed. The first half of the book is a man warning an exile community about a catastrophe that hasn't happened yet, and that most of his hearers believe won't.
What deportation was | Imperial policy and not a stampede: selective removal of a ruling class, craftsmen and royal family, resettlement in communities that kept some property and internal structure, and a client administration left behind. It was designed to take the head off a state while keeping it productive.
Structure | Chapters 1–24, judgment on Jerusalem before the fall. Chapters 25–32, oracles against the surrounding nations. Chapters 33–48, after the fall: restoration, the shepherds, the bones and a measured temple. The fall of the city is the hinge, and the book is built around it.
Dating system | The book dates its oracles by years counted from King Jehoiachin's exile, and not by Babylonian regnal years. INTERPRETATION: A community that dates its documents from the year its king was taken, and not from the reign of the power that took him, is choosing whose time it lives in.
box: Don't reduce the book to a mobile God
The departure of the glory from the temple is central and very useful for Dominion, and it isn't the whole book. Ezekiel is also about personal accountability, failed leadership, the ethics of prophetic speech and a detailed plan for rebuilding, and it ends with architecture more than travel.
/end
prompt: What an exile community keeps, and what Dominion would take first | fill

@page rh="EZEKIEL · 2 OF 5 · THE CALL, THE GLORY, THE PERFORMANCES" m="EZEK 1; EZEK 2–3; EZEK 4–5; EZEK 8–11; EZEK 12; EZEK 24.15–27; EZEK 43"
# A prophet used as an instrument
Ezekiel mostly doesn't speak. He's made into a demonstration, positioned, restricted, silenced and bereaved so his body becomes the message. No other prophetic book does this on this scale.
rec:
The call and the wheels · 1–3 | A storm from the north, four living creatures with four faces each, wheels that move without turning with eyes on the rims, and above them the likeness of a throne with the likeness of a human form. The vocabulary is hedged at every turn, likeness, appearance, something like, because what he saw can't be reported plainly. He's handed a scroll and told to eat it, and it tastes like honey.
The glory leaves · 8–11, returns · 43 | He's shown what's being done inside the temple, and the divine presence moves, from the inner sanctuary to the threshold, to the east gate, out of the city. It's a staged withdrawal, recorded like a route. Thirty-two chapters later it comes back by the same gate to a rebuilt house. INTERPRETATION: The building was never the point. The presence was.
The performances · 4–5, 12 | He lies on one side and then the other for a counted number of days. He eats a measured siege ration. He shaves his head and beard and divides the hair. He digs through a wall and carries out a packed bag at dusk, like a refugee. Public, humiliating, sustained, and commissioned as prophecy.
The death of his wife · 24.15–27 | She's called the delight of his eyes. He's told in advance she'll die and told not to mourn her in public, no weeping and no rites, because his withheld grief is the sign of what's coming to the city. She dies, and he does as he was told. The text names her only by her relation to him, gives her no words and uses her death as an illustration. Whatever else the book is, it does that.
prompt: What the Empire would do with a licensed prophet whose body is the Broadcast | fill

@page rh="EZEKIEL · 3 OF 5 · CHAPTERS 16 AND 23" m="EZEK 16; EZEK 23; HOS 1–3 (COMPARE); WEEMS, BATTERED LOVE; GALAMBUSH, JERUSALEM IN THE BOOK OF EZEKIEL; TKC: DOM · MEN"
note: Two extended prophetic allegories that describe women's bodies in explicit sexual terms and present stripping, public exposure, mutilation and killing as deserved punishment for infidelity. This page doesn't quote them. You can work it later, or not at all, and the spread still functions.
# The metaphor that uses a woman's body
rec:
What they are | Chapter 16 casts Jerusalem as a foundling who is raised, adorned, married and unfaithful. Chapter 23 casts Samaria and Jerusalem as two sisters whose political alliances are pictured as sexual pursuit of foreign soldiers.
The argument | Alliance with imperial powers is pictured as adultery, and the coming destruction as a husband's punishment of a wife, carried out by her former lovers with divine authorization. The violence is the argument.
Why it matters | For centuries these chapters were preached with the gendered violence treated as decoration on a spiritual point. A large body of modern scholarship, feminist criticism in particular, argues the imagery can't be incidental and that its long use shapes how women hear divine judgment. It's serious scholarship and not a fringe complaint.
Readings on offer | CONTESTED: The metaphor indicts a city and its leaders, and not women. It draws on treaty language, where infidelity is the standard charge. The shock is the intended effect. The imagery does harm whatever its intent. These can all be true together, and none of them makes the text safe.
The companion's rule | Name what the text does. Don't quote it to shock. Don't soften it into "unfaithfulness." Don't treat centuries of preaching on it as a neutral fact.
empire: Why this is directly TKC
POSSIBLE: Dominion already measures a household's loyalty on a woman's body and stages exposure as correction. It has a scriptural warrant at length, in a major prophet. The question is whether it quotes these chapters in public or keeps them for the closed catechism.
/end
prompt: My own position. Write it down once, here, so the manuscript stays consistent | fill

@page rh="EZEKIEL · 4 OF 5 · GUILT, SHEPHERDS, BONES, BLUEPRINT" m="EZEK 18; EXOD 20.5; LAM 5.7; EZEK 34; EZEK 37.1–14; EZEK 38–39; EZEK 40–48"
# After the city falls
rec:
Whose fault · 18 | The chapter quotes a proverb the exiles are using, the parents have eaten sour grapes and the children's teeth are set on edge, and rejects it. The person who sins is the one who bears it, and someone who turns isn't held to their record. Read it against the commandment's language of punishment visited on later generations (Exod 20.5), against Lamentations' complaint that the children bear their parents' guilt (Lam 5.7), and against Ezekiel's own account of a whole city destroyed together. The Bible doesn't speak with one voice here, and the disagreement is the material.
The shepherds · 34 | An indictment of rulers who fed themselves on the flock, wore the wool, ate the fat, didn't strengthen the weak, heal the sick or search for the lost, and ruled with force. God removes them and takes the work personally, searching, gathering, feeding, binding up and judging between fat sheep and lean. The chapter then promises one shepherd, "my servant David." The office is emptied, worked directly and refilled on God's terms. INTERPRETATION: It's the most quotable anti-leadership passage in the prophets and the hardest for an institution to circulate safely.
The valley of dry bones · 37 | He's set in a valley of bones and asked whether they can live, and he answers that God knows. The bones come together and breath enters them. The text interprets itself. The bones are the whole house of Israel, a people who believe they're finished. In context it's about national restoration. Later traditions read it as individual resurrection, and that reading has its own history. Keep the two apart.
Gog and Magog · 38–39 | A vast future invasion from the north, defeated. Later apocalyptic writing and modern political preaching have both mined these chapters for the enemy's identity. Note the pattern without adopting it, since naming a final enemy is a durable political technology.
The measured temple · 40–48 | Nine chapters of specification: gates, courts, chambers, altar, priestly duties, festival calendar, a river flowing out of the sanctuary and a redivided land. A priest in exile answers catastrophe with a building plan and a rota. CONTESTED: Whether it's a blueprint, a vision or a protest against the temple that failed. INTERPRETATION: What it plainly is, is hope written as an ordinance.
prompt: Does Dominion inherit guilt by household or by person, and does it say the same thing twice? | fill

